---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const inlaws = [
  ["Fucci / Fuccio", 32], ["d'Onofrio", 30], ["Magliano", 16], ["d'Ambrosio", 15],
  ["Caserta", 14], ["De Lucia", 14], ["Esposito", 12], ["Maione", 10],
  ["Petrarca", 10], ["Cimmino", 6], ["Castaldo", 5], ["Crisci", 4], ["Girardi", 4],
];
const shared = ["Maione", "Crisci", "De Lucia", "Cimmino"];
---
<Base title="Zampiello">
  <h1>Zampiello</h1>
  <p class="lede">The rarest name in this family by a wide margin — and, it turns out, the name of one
  village.</p>

  <h2>About 123 people in the world</h2>
  <p>Forebears counts roughly <strong>123 bearers of Zampiello worldwide</strong>: 84 in the United
  States, <strong>37 in Italy</strong>, 2 in France. It is the 1,628,521st commonest surname on earth.
  Set beside Falco's 46,749, the difference is not one of degree.</p>
  <p>A name that rare is, in practice, a single family. And the records say where it lives.</p>

  <h2>It is an Arpaia name</h2>
  <p><strong>226 Zampiello records</strong> were recovered from the civil registers of the province of
  Benevento, running from <strong>1861 to 1915</strong>. Every one of them is registered at
  <strong>Arpaia</strong> — the comune of some eight hundred people, four kilometres from Arienzo across
  the provincial border, where Francesca Zampiello was born in 1928.</p>
  <p>The given names repeat as tightly as the surname: Francesco (14), Giuseppe (10), Antonio (10),
  Caterina (10), Maria Grazia (8), Michele, Marta, Filomena, Raffaele, Tommaso, Angelo.</p>

  <h2>Who they married</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Records</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{inlaws.map(([n,c]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="mono">{c}</td>
        <td>{shared.includes(n) ? <span class="chip doc">also an Arienzo Falco in-law name</span> : null}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The two families' worlds already overlapped</h2>
  <p>Carmine Falco married Francesca Zampiello at Arpaia on 15 January 1955, and it is easy to read that as two
  strangers meeting. The registers suggest otherwise. <strong>Maione, Crisci, De Lucia and
  Cimmino</strong> all appear as in-law names in <em>both</em> networks — among the Falco of Arienzo and
  among the Zampiello of Arpaia — a century before that marriage.</p>
  <p>Costanza <strong>Maione</strong> married Raffaele Falco at Arienzo in 1897. Raffaella
  <strong>Maione</strong> appears as a Zampiello mother at Arpaia in 1899. Whether these are one kindred
  is untested, and the surname alone does not prove it — but four shared names across two villages four
  kilometres apart is not nothing.</p>
  <p class="small">Stated as a pattern, not a conclusion. It is exactly the kind of claim this archive
  exists to be careful about.</p>

  <h2>Francesca's mother — the name this page was missing</h2>
  <p>Francesca Zampiello's parents were <P n="Angelo Zampiello" bold={true} /> (31 August 1895 – 25 April
  1972) and <strong>GIUSEPPA FERRARA</strong>. <strong>Until 2026 this page did not name her once</strong>
  — an archive built outward from the Falco surname had followed the Zampiello men and let the woman
  who bore the Zampiello daughters fall out of the account. That is now corrected, and the omission is
  recorded rather than quietly patched.</p>
  <p>Three Italian civil records name her, and <strong>all three spell her FERRARO</strong> where the
  family papers write <em>Ferrara</em>: the two 1935 death entries for her infant sons, and her
  daughter Tommasina's marriage act of 26 August 1939. She and Angelo had at least five children —
  Tommasina (1921), Francesca (1928), Caterina, and <strong>two sons, Angelo and Aniello, both born in
  1935 and both dead on 26 April 1935</strong>.</p>
  <p><a href={u("/ferrara")}><strong>The Ferrara &rarr;</strong></a></p>

  <h2>What is still missing</h2>
  <p class="small">Also listed, with everything else outstanding, at <a href="../open-questions/">Open questions</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Angelo Zampiello's own birth record</strong> (31 August 1895) has not been recovered.
    FamilySearch's search caps deep paging at about 1,100 results and treats <em>Zampiello</em> as
    interchangeable with <em>Zampella</em>, <em>Zampillo</em> and <em>Campiello</em>, which floods the
    result set. His parents are therefore still unnamed.</li>
    <li><strong>His war — corroborated, and by the family's own paper.</strong> The certificate was in
    the family scans all along, filed under the wrong caption. The <strong>Ministero dell'Africa
    Italiana</strong> conferred the <strong>Croce al Merito di Guerra</strong> by decree of 14 September
    1938 on <em>«all'operaio <strong>Zampiello Angelo di Tommaso</strong>»</em>, under the royal decree
    extending the award to those who took part in operations in East Africa <strong>from 3 October
    1935</strong> — the day Italy crossed into Ethiopia.
    <br/>Two things follow. He was there as an <strong><em>operaio</em>, a workman</strong>, not a
    soldier: Italy sent tens of thousands of labourers to build the roads that campaign ran on, and the
    1936 decree exists to extend a military award to them. And the certificate names
    <strong>his father, Tommaso Zampiello</strong> — a generation this archive did not have.
    <br/><em>The part still unproven is the bravery.</em> A merit cross is for service in a theatre of
    operations; an act of gallantry would ordinarily carry a <em>Medaglia al Valor Militare</em>. The
    family's account of lives saved may be true and separately awarded, or it may be their gloss on this
    same certificate. See <a href={u("/documents")}>the certificate</a>.</li>
    <li>His son Angelo, born at Arpaia in 1935, <strong>died there the same year</strong>, on 26 April.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
